#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# integer_exponent

#% text
# `integer_exponent(n)` gives the number of trailing decimal zeros of `n`.

#% text
# `integer_exponent(n, b)` gives the largest `k` with `b^k` dividing `n`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `n` is a non-zero integer and `b` an integer at least 2.

#% item
# `integer_exponent` is listable in its first argument.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
integer_exponent(12000)
#% output
#= 3

#% cell
integer_exponent(48, 2)
#% output
#= 4

#% cell
integer_exponent(81, 3)
#% output
#= 4

#% cell
integer_exponent(7, 2)
#% output
#= 0

#% subsection
# Applications

#% text
# The number of trailing zeros of `100!`:

#% cell
integer_exponent(factorial(100))
#% output
#= 24

#% text
# The power of 2 in each of 1 .. 16:

#% cell
integer_exponent(range(16), 2)
#% output
#= [0, 1, 0, 2, 0, 1, 0, 3, 0, 1, 0, 2, 0, 1, 0, 4]

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `factor_integer`, `integer_digits`, `mod`
